Anarchy is multi-licensed because it composes code derived from multiple
upstream projects with different (compatible) licenses.

The exact license that applies to each file is recorded in its containing
crate / package manifest (`Cargo.toml` `license = ...` or `package.json`
`license`). The map below is informative.

Component                                License (SPDX)
---------------------------------------- -----------------------------------------------
apps/blockchain/node/                    GPL-3.0-or-later WITH Classpath-exception-2.0
  └─ matches its sc-* / substrate-* deps
apps/blockchain/runtime/                 Apache-2.0
apps/blockchain/pallets/*/               Apache-2.0
  └─ matches frame-* / sp-* deps
apps/blockchain/primitives/              Apache-2.0
apps/storage-node/                       MIT OR Apache-2.0
apps/frontend/                           MIT
packages/wasm-engine/                    MIT OR Apache-2.0
packages/kzg-constants/                  MIT OR Apache-2.0
scripts/                                 MIT
docs/                                    CC-BY-4.0 (prose) / MIT (snippets)

Full license texts:

  LICENSE-MIT         — MIT License
  LICENSE-APACHE-2.0  — Apache License, Version 2.0
  LICENSE-GPL-3.0     — GNU General Public License v3 + Classpath-exception-2.0

Why this split?

  The Substrate / Polkadot SDK upstream uses two licenses depending on layer:
    - frame-* / sp-* (runtime / primitives)  → Apache-2.0
    - sc-*  / node binaries                  → GPL-3.0-or-later
                                                WITH Classpath-exception-2.0

  Distributing the node binary under MIT would be incompatible because MIT
  grants rights that GPL does not. To respect both upstreams, the node
  inherits the more restrictive GPL (with classpath exception), the runtime
  / pallets stay Apache-2.0, and the rest of the project (storage daemon,
  frontend, Wasm crypto engine) is permissively licensed.

